-- title:
--   Lemma 7.3.2 — Hamiltonians in the commutant generate a Lie algebra acting only on the degeneracies (corrected: $\mathcal L\subseteq\bigoplus_J\mathcal L_{n_J}\otimes I_{d_J}$, not a direct sum)
-- statement:
--   Let $A=\{W^\dagger(\bigoplus_J I_{n_J}\otimes X_J)W : X_J\in M_{d_J}\}\cong\bigoplus_{J\in\mathcal J}I_{n_J}\otimes M_{d_J}$ be an OSR (or SME) algebra in decomposed form, with $W$ unitary and $n_J,d_J\ge1$, let $K\in\mathcal J$ with irrep projector $P_K$, and let $S$ be a set of Hamiltonians (Hermitian operators) each of which commutes with every element of $A$. Then:
--   1. every $H\in S$ acts on information encoded into the degeneracy of the $K$-th irrep without taking it out of that irrep: $HP_K=P_KH$, there is a Hermitian $n_K\times n_K$ matrix $Y_H$ with $HP_K=W^\dagger(0\oplus\cdots\oplus(Y_H\otimes I_{d_K})\oplus\cdots\oplus0)W$, and $e^{-iHt}P_K=W^\dagger(0\oplus\cdots\oplus(e^{-iY_Ht}\otimes I_{d_K})\oplus\cdots\oplus0)W$ for every real $t$;
--   2. the real Lie algebra $\mathcal L$ generated by $\{iH : H\in S\}$ (Lie brackets and real linear combinations) has the reducible structure
--   $$
--   \mathcal L\subseteq\Bigl\{W^\dagger\Bigl(\bigoplus_{J\in\mathcal J}Y_J\otimes I_{d_J}\Bigr)W : Y_J\in M_{n_J}\Bigr\},
--   $$
--   that is, $\mathcal L\subseteq\bigoplus_J\mathcal L_{n_J}\otimes I_{d_J}$, where $\mathcal L_{n_J}=\{Y_J : X\in\mathcal L\}$, the restriction of $\mathcal L$ to the degeneracy of the $J$-th irrep, is a (perhaps further reducible) Lie algebra of $n_J\times n_J$ matrices, and $I_{d_J}$ is the identity on the $d_J$-dimensional irrep factor.
--
--   The lemma is the Lie-algebraic form of Lemma 7.3.1: whatever a set of commutant Hamiltonians generates under the bracket still acts only on the degeneracies, so encoded information never leaves its irrep.
--
--   **Formalization Note** "Generate" is `LieSubalgebra.lieSpan ℝ` of $\{iH\}$ with the commutator bracket (§7.3.3 contrasts it with the associative algebra). The commutant is `Set.centralizer`.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - correction: (7.9) L ≅ ⊕_J L_{n_J} ⊗ I_{d_J}, a direct sum of Lie algebras → every element of L has the form W†(⊕_J Y_J ⊗ I_{d_J})W, i.e. L ⊆ ⊕_J L_{n_J} ⊗ I_{d_J} with L_{n_J} the restriction of L to block J; reason: two irreps with n_J = d_J = 1 (A the diagonal 2 × 2 matrices) and S = {diag(1, 2)} give L = ℝ·i·diag(1, 2), which is not the direct sum ℝi ⊕ ℝi of its block restrictions (i·diag(1, 0) is in the sum but not in L)
--   - reading: "will act on this encoded information without taking this encoded information out of the Kth irrep" → for every H ∈ S: H P_K = P_K H, H P_K = W†(0 ⊕ (Y ⊗ I_{d_K}) ⊕ 0)W with Y Hermitian, and e^{−iHt} P_K = W†(0 ⊕ (e^{−iYt} ⊗ I_{d_K}) ⊕ 0)W for all real t; reason: the block form (7.2) that Lemma 7.3.1's proof invokes, for the operator and for its evolution
--   - encoding: "S will generate a Lie algebra L" → the real Lie span of {iH : H ∈ S} with the matrix commutator; reason: Hamiltonians H generate unitaries e^{−iHt}; §7.3.3 specifies Lie bracket and linear combination
--   - encoding: A ≅ ⊕_J I_{n_J} ⊗ M_{d_J} → A = {W† (⊕_J I_{n_J} ⊗ X_J) W} for an explicit unitary W; reason: the basis change of Theorem 5.4.1 made explicit; same instances
--   - encoding: irreps J ∈ 𝒥 of Theorem 5.4.1 (each irrep present, with a degeneracy n_J and a dimension d_J) → hypotheses ∀ J, 0 < n J and ∀ J, 0 < d J; reason: the book's irreps and degeneracies are nonzero; an empty block would contribute nothing to any operator, so no instance changes
-- source:
--   Bacon, Decoherence, Control, and Symmetry in Quantum Computers, arXiv:quant-ph/0305025v1, §7.3.3, Lemma 7.3.2, eq. (7.9), p.103 (PDF p.120); introduced at the end of p.102 (PDF p.119)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_BaconThesis_Commutant_blockC
import Definitions.Def_BaconThesis_Commutant_algebraA
import Definitions.Def_BaconThesis_Commutant_irrepProj
import Definitions.Def_BaconThesis_Commutant_lieGen

attribute [local instance 100] LieRing.ofAssociativeRing

namespace BaconThesis.Commutant

/-- **Lemma 7.3.2 (Hamiltonians in the commutant generate a Lie algebra acting only on the
degeneracies; corrected (7.9)).**
Bacon, *Decoherence, Control, and Symmetry in Quantum Computers*, arXiv:quant-ph/0305025v1,
§7.3.3, Lemma 7.3.2, p.103 (PDF p.120): "Suppose one is given an OSR or SME algebra
`A ≅ ⊕_{J∈𝒥} I_{n_J} ⊗ M_{d_J}` and information has been encoded into the degeneracy of the `K`th
irrep. A set of Hamiltonians `S` each of which commutes with all of the elements of `A` will act on
this encoded information without taking this encoded information out of the `K`th irrep.
Furthermore this set of Hamiltonians `S` will generate a Lie algebra `L` which has a reducible
structure of the form `L ≅ ⊕_{J∈𝒥} L_{n_J} ⊗ I_{d_J}`, (7.9) where `L_{n_J}` is a (perhaps further
reducible) `n_J` dimensional Lie algebra and `I_{d_J}` represents the identity action on a `d_J`
dimensional space."

Let `A = {W† (⊕_J I_{n_J} ⊗ X_J) W}` as in `algebraA` (`W` unitary, `n_J, d_J ≥ 1`), let `K` be an
irrep label with projector `P_K`, and let `S` be a set of Hermitian operators each commuting with
every element of `A`. Then
1. every `H ∈ S` satisfies `H P_K = P_K H`, acts on the `K`-th irrep as `Y_H ⊗ I_{d_K}` for a
   Hermitian `Y_H` on the degeneracy, and its evolution satisfies
   `e^{−iHt} P_K = W† (0 ⊕ ⋯ ⊕ (e^{−iY_H t} ⊗ I_{d_K}) ⊕ ⋯ ⊕ 0) W` for every real `t`;
2. every element `X` of the real Lie algebra `L` generated by `{iH : H ∈ S}` (`lieGen S`) has the
   form `X = W† (⊕_J Y_J ⊗ I_{d_J}) W` for some `n_J × n_J` matrices `Y_J`; that is,
   `L ⊆ ⊕_J L_{n_J} ⊗ I_{d_J}`, where `L_{n_J} = {Y_J : X ∈ L}` (the restriction of `L` to the
   degeneracy of the `J`-th irrep) is a real Lie algebra of `n_J × n_J` matrices.

**Formalization Note.** Correction of (7.9): read as a direct sum of Lie algebras, (7.9) is false
(two irreps with `n_J = d_J = 1`, `S = {diag(1, 2)}`: `L = ℝ · i diag(1, 2)` is not
`ℝi ⊕ ℝi`); clause 2 is the reducible block structure the lemma asserts. "Generate" is the real
Lie span with the matrix commutator (§7.3.3), not the associative algebra. -/
theorem lemma_7_3_2 {𝒥 ι : Type} [Fintype 𝒥] [DecidableEq 𝒥] [Fintype ι] [DecidableEq ι]
    (n d : 𝒥 → ℕ) (hn : ∀ J, 0 < n J) (hd : ∀ J, 0 < d J)
    (W : Matrix (Σ J : 𝒥, Fin (n J) × Fin (d J)) ι ℂ)
    (hW₁ : W.conjTranspose * W = 1) (hW₂ : W * W.conjTranspose = 1)
    (K : 𝒥) (S : Set (Matrix ι ι ℂ))
    (hS : ∀ H ∈ S, H.IsHermitian ∧ H ∈ Set.centralizer (algebraA n d W)) :
    (∀ H ∈ S, H * irrepProj n d W K = irrepProj n d W K * H ∧
      ∃ Y : Matrix (Fin (n K)) (Fin (n K)) ℂ, Y.IsHermitian ∧
        H * irrepProj n d W K =
          W.conjTranspose *
            blockC n d (Pi.single (M := fun J => Matrix (Fin (n J)) (Fin (n J)) ℂ) K Y) * W ∧
        ∀ t : ℝ, NormedSpace.exp ((-(t : ℂ) * Complex.I) • H) * irrepProj n d W K =
          W.conjTranspose *
            blockC n d (Pi.single (M := fun J => Matrix (Fin (n J)) (Fin (n J)) ℂ) K
              (NormedSpace.exp ((-(t : ℂ) * Complex.I) • Y))) * W) ∧
    ∀ X ∈ lieGen S, ∃ Y : (J : 𝒥) → Matrix (Fin (n J)) (Fin (n J)) ℂ,
      X = W.conjTranspose * blockC n d Y * W := by sorry

end BaconThesis.Commutant
